  "text": "Question nine. When should you use a spreadsheet, and when SQL or a database? A spreadsheet wins for quick exploration, small data, and something to hand a stakeholder. Move to SQL when the data outgrows it, well before a million rows, when the work has to be repeatable and audited, or when it joins several tables. If you redo the same manual steps every week, it belongs in SQL. Question ten. What are the main risks of analysis in a spreadsheet? Manual steps nobody can rerun. Formulas that break quietly when rows move. No schema: one revenue typed as text drops out of the sum, and the total reads ten seventy instead of twelve twenty. And big files that crawl, then crash.",
